# Overview
#
# In grammar an expletive is a word that fills a slot in a sentence
# without adding meaning. The term is unrelated to the everyday sense of
# a swear word. An expletive opener fills the subject slot with `there`
# or `it` and moves the real subject later (`There is a flag that
# disables the cache`, `it is possible that the cache is stale`). The
# reader must read past the opener to learn what the sentence is about.
#
# The fix makes the real subject the subject of the sentence (`The
# --no-cache flag disables the cache`, `the cache might be stale`).
# Sentences built as `What X does is Y`, which grammar calls
# pseudo-clefts (`What the hook does is skip the file`), and `the reason
# is that` delay the real subject the same way, so the rule reports them
# too. The rule sits at Recommended, because the `there is` branches
# cannot tell a `that` that opens a description from a `that` that
# introduces a noun.
#
# Expression
#
# `there` lists `there is` with its forms for tense and number, such as
# `there were` and `there's`. `there is` counts only with a relative
# pronoun (`that`, `which`, or `who` opening a description of the noun
# before it) one to three words later. A list picks its longest match
# and never backtracks, so each gap length is its own alternative.
# ProseNotOnly writes its `but` tail the same way. `what` counts when
# `does`, `do`, or `did` and then `is` follow one to three words later
# (`What the hook does is`), and each gap length is again its own
# alternative.
#
# `it is`, `it was`, and `it's` count before `that` when one word sits
# between them and Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks that word `ADJ`
# (an adjective), as in `it is possible that`.
#
# False Positives
#
# Without a relative pronoun, `there is a lock` states that a thing
# exists, so it passes.
#
# Related Rules
#
# `it is advisable that` stays out, because the Recommended
# ProseObligation reports `it is advisable`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 121 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 19 of 20 sampled
# hits rewrote with the real subject first. In the one misfire, `that`
# was a determiner, a word that introduces a noun, such as `the`, `a`,
# or `this` (`there's no value for that key`).
expr there [(there is), (there are), (there was), (there were), there's]
expr rel [that, which, who]
expr does [does, do, did]
expr main [
    (@there * @rel),
    (@there * * @rel),
    (@there * * * @rel),
    ([(it is), (it was), it's] <ADJ, !advisable> that),
    (what * @does is),
    (what * * @does is),
    (what * * * @does is),
    (the reason [is, was] that)
]
let message "Expletive opener. Make the real subject the subject of the sentence."
let description "Flags `there is ... that`, `it is ... that`, and similar openers that delay the real subject."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The --no-cache flag disables the cache."
allows "There is a lock."
allows "The cache might be stale."
allows "It is advisable that you pin the version."
